Subject: [PATCH] dwc2: bring host mode up after a single IDDIG debounce on role-switch platforms
Upstream-Status: Pending

An accessory-initiated role switch on the Carlinkit Mini Ultra (Ingenic
X1600, DWC2 core 3.00a, IDDIG filter present and no bypass) currently
costs two debounce periods before the root hub port is powered:

  [1.006232] force_mode(host): GUSBCFG 4000140f -> 2000140f
  [1.106381] dwc2_conn_id_status_change(): GOTGCTL=0024007c    <- +100ms, core is host
  [1.106448] wait_for_mode(host): start GINTSTS=14000020        <- core reset, device again
  [1.206952] wait_for_mode(host): done after 100 ms              <- +200ms
  [1.207129] Init: Port Power applied, HPRT0=00001000

The first period is the hardware filter reacting to the force-mode
write; nothing to be done about that on this core. The second one is
self-inflicted: dwc2_conn_id_status_change() calls dwc2_core_init(false),
which soft-resets the core, and as dwc2_core_reset() documents, a soft
reset puts the core back into device mode until the filter has run once
more. dwc2_core_reset() then waits for that, so host init is delayed by
another 100ms.

Whether a head unit tolerates that decides catplay-firmware issue #1. A
Continental NAC Wave 2 (Toyota Proace Verso) does not: with the vendor
firmware, which forces host mode 18ms after the role-switch request and
has its gadget disabled 100ms after that, the head unit is enumerated
as a device 300ms later. With this driver the port is powered ~200ms
after the force and no device is ever seen on it - 24 HPRT0 samples over
six seconds, CONNSTS=0 in every one, in every capture. When the dongle
finally returns to device mode the head unit re-enumerates it at once,
so it was back in host mode by then, retrying from the top. The
maintainer's own reading of the issue is the same: "role switch timings
being again on the edge of what car tolerates" and "another 100ms to
cut in dwc2.c".

Cut it. On role-switch platforms (dr_mode OTG with a usb-role-switch
property, the same condition patches 0039 and 0043 key on) the host path
of dwc2_conn_id_status_change() now calls dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true),
i.e. without the soft reset. What host mode needs is rewritten
regardless: dwc2_core_init() reprograms GAHBCFG, GUSBCFG and GINTMSK,
dwc2_hcd_start() -> dwc2_core_host_init() reprograms and flushes the
FIFOs, halts all host channels and installs the host interrupt mask.
Device-mode endpoint state is inert once CURMODE reads host, the B path
of the same function still resets on the way back to device mode (which
is also where dwc2_clear_fifo_map() matters), and the GOTGCTL session
overrides that carry this platform's sessions survive a reset anyway,
so skipping it changes nothing about them. initial_setup=true also
passes select_phy=true to dwc2_phy_init(), and dwc2_hs_phy_init() only
writes GUSBCFG (and only then resets) if the PHY selection differs from
what is already programmed - it does not.

Expected result on this hardware: "Init: Port Power applied" ~100ms
after force_mode(host) instead of ~200ms, and no "wait_for_mode(host)"
pair in between. With the 20ms settle time g_iphone now keeps between
the head unit's ACK and the force, the whole sequence lands where the
vendor firmware's does.

Not compiled, not run: written against the letux-6.18.y tree at
a3d00c045d89 with 0021, 0022, 0030, 0039, 0043 and 0044 applied.
---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c | 46 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
@@ -3226,8 +3226,52 @@
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
 		hsotg->op_state = OTG_STATE_A_HOST;
-		/* Initialize the Core for Host mode */
-		dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
+
+		/*
+		 * Initialize the Core for Host mode.
+		 *
+		 * On role-switch platforms (dr_mode OTG, no external ID pin,
+		 * mode changes come from GUSBCFG force bits written by
+		 * dwc2_drd_role_sw_set()) we are here because that write
+		 * raised the Connector ID Status Change interrupt - after
+		 * one IDDIG debounce period (100ms on cores older than
+		 * 3.10a, which have no filter bypass). The core is already
+		 * in host mode, see the loop above.
+		 *
+		 * dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false) would now soft-reset the core.
+		 * Per dwc2_core_reset() a soft reset drops the core back into
+		 * device mode until the debounce filter has run once more, so
+		 * host bring-up ends up paying that 100ms twice, and a head
+		 * unit that swapped roles on our ACK sits as a device with
+		 * nobody driving a bus reset for the whole time. On a NAC
+		 * Wave 2 that is the difference between the vendor firmware
+		 * (port powered ~100ms after forcing host mode, head unit
+		 * enumerated) and this driver (~200ms, head unit gone).
+		 *
+		 * Skip the reset there. Everything host mode depends on is
+		 * rewritten regardless: dwc2_core_init() reprograms GAHBCFG,
+		 * GUSBCFG and GINTMSK from scratch, dwc2_hcd_start() ->
+		 * dwc2_core_host_init() reprograms and flushes the FIFOs,
+		 * halts every host channel and re-enables the host interrupt
+		 * set. Device-mode endpoint state is dead once CURMODE is
+		 * host, and the B path of this function still resets on the
+		 * way back to device mode, which also clears the FIFO map.
+		 * The GOTGCTL session overrides survive the reset anyway, so
+		 * nothing about the override-driven session changes either.
+		 *
+		 * initial_setup=true is exactly "do not reset, (re)select the
+		 * PHY": dwc2_hs_phy_init() only touches GUSBCFG if the PHY
+		 * selection differs from what is programmed, which it never
+		 * does at this point.
+		 */
+		if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+		    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch")) {
+			dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+				 "connId A: role-switch platform, host init without core reset\n");
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true);
+		} else {
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
+		}
 		dwc2_enable_global_interrupts(hsotg);
 		dev_info(hsotg->dev,
 			 "connId A: core reinit done, mode=%s, starting HCD\n",
